Date: Tue, 5 Oct 1993 14:28:26 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Extortion Countermeasures in Crypto-Anarchy

Tim May writes:

>1. After an initial period of confusion, some people would send
>extortion messages out to many people (if one victim is good, a
>thousand are better....best of all is the entire community).

Waah!! [interpolate manic laughter here]

I don't have the article to hand, but a few months ago I read in
one of the more reputable British newspapers that an enterprising
Japanese businessman had done just that.  He sent out 4000-odd
mailmerged blackmail letters to names picked out of the Tokyo
telephone directory at random.  Then the 4001st recipient shopped him
to the police.

What's interesting about this is that about 90 of his victims
payed up.  Presumably a good 2.5% of the telephone-owning
population of Tokyo have something to hide.  Compare this to the
0.025% of the sample cohort who had a sufficiently clear
conscience to go to the police!

Now, if _I_ had been in his shoes I'd have spent the money hiring
PI's or knowbots to figure out what those cash cows were so afraid of,
so that round #2 of blackmail material could be more specific and
demand more money. Think of it as a self-selection process for
blackmail victims. But of course, my highly-developed moral
sensibilities preclude me from ever taking advantage of such a born
mark ...


-- Charlie

