From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: ethics and morals

Jeff Fabijanic writes:

> Hmm. The way that I understand the terms, a *moral* is a personally
> held principle concerning the "rightness" or "goodness" of a behavior.
> An *ethic* is a moral principle that is held to be true within a
> particular social framework. Either can be justified from intellectual,
> religious, or other points of view.

I'm curious to see why you interpret the words that way, because their 
etymologies do not concur with your view:  "moral" comes from a Latin root 
meaning "custom", whereas "ethical" comes from a Greek root which, though it 
also has the sense "custom" or "character," is more typically used to mean 
"behavior" (ethology being the study of animal behavior, for example).

More to the point, as Harry points out, is the modern connotation of each word.
"Moral" tends to carry a religious or "I know what's right for you" sense that 
"ethical" does not.  For example, the Catholic Church would consider a group 
marriage immoral, but probably not unethical.

So I think Harry's point is well taken, and we should use "ethical" instead of 
"moral," since we are more concerned with individual behavior than with custom.

-- Simon! 



