Date: Tue, 5 Oct 93 12:00:41 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Business Expertise & Crypto-Anarchy

I remarked on the lack of business expertise on the cypherpunks
and Extropians lists, and how that holds back the crypto-anarchy movement.
I was corrected by folks pointing out their involvement with investing
in hi-tech businesses or marketing hi-tech products.

To be more specific, I know of only one person on the 
Extropians or cypherpunks list with experience on the business side
(management, marketing, etc.) of banking, and none with experience
in business-scale gambling, human resources, or billing (the four biggest
crypto-anarchy markets, in my estimation: private banking, online
gambling, crypto-telecommuting, and secure billing).  This rarity 
extends to the net in general.  There might be a few such folks who hang 
out on misc.invest or rec.gambling, but all I've ever seen there are the 
customers.  Making or selling technology is not the same thing as running 
a financial services business; not even close.

I'd enjoy being proven wrong; if any reader has such expertise 
I'd love to hear from you.

The cultural gulf between the politically motivated crypto hackers and 
the business world, the tendency to artificially separate business from
politics when in fact the structure of one highly depends on the
structure of the other (cf. by Libertarian Realpolitik essay), and
the tendency of making "trade secrets" out of preliminary, amateur 
business speculations that would profit both future politics and
business if they were discussed more widely, may explain in large part 
why the crypto-anarchy movement is "stalled", as Tim May described 
at the last cypherpunks meeting -- a big burst of effort writing and 
deploying anon remailers last fall, and not much written since.  

This may also explain why so much time is spent
discussing the negative aspects of crypto-anarchy (extortion, child
porn, etc.) and the political aspects (Clipper, PGP crackdown, etc.)
and so little is spent on the positive aspects (private banking, 
reduction of government control over our lives, etc.)
Crypto-anarchy is being treated as some sort of medieval religious
debate where the most important thing is to line one's rhetorical ducks
in a row ("by playing devil's advocate now, we become prepared to fend
off arguments citing extortion, child porn, etc. later").  Such
rhetorical games are valuable, but they are hardly the most
important activity.

More important is the Realpolitik of actually building the world we 
want, and funding its construction.  Both the hacking and the public
debate will tend to follow the money.  If I have some of my money in 
an offshore crypto-bank I'm much more motivated to fund opposition to
the crypto crackdown.  If I'm earning a salary or building equity
in a rapidly growing company I'll be much more motivated to write
digital mixes, PGP GUIs, etc.  If we had such banks right now, or 
crypto services for those evil gamblers or drug runners or Blacknetters, 
Phil Zimmerman's defense fund coffers would be overflowing.  Thus, meeting 
the political goals of crypto-anarchy is primarily a business proposition, 
a matter of creating and selling of useful services.  If we don't create 
viable crypto-anarchy business models to get a chunk of the world's
resources behind us, all the political rhetoric in the world isn't 
going to do much good.

It's also important to note that the rhetoric needed to convince
a businessman of the viability of crypto-anarchy is very different
the the rhetoric needed to convince politicians or the voting public.
I see this all the time in sci.space, people making "business cases"
that might be quite effective if used lobbying for government pork,
but which would be ludicrous to any knowledgeable private-sector
businessman.  We have a huge culture of space fans whose very mindset 
is political, their vision sterile when it comes to business viability.

On the cypherpunks list we worry about public opinion, but what about
the opinion of the entrepreneurs even now establishing Internet
commerce standards for privacy, billing, etc.?  Their opinion is 
vastly more important than Joe Public's or even the Congresscritters'.
Nor will we convince the entrepreneurs with moral arguments about
the good or evil effects of crypto-anarchy; rather we must point
out its vast potential for expanding their bottom line.  

I fear the "government lobbying rot" that has corrupted space fandom 
might also eat away at crypto-anarchy, unless we somehow infuse 
the movement with new blood of real business expertise in banking, 
gambling, etc.

Nick Szabo					szabo@netcom.com

