Date: Tue, 5 Oct 93 13:05:24 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com (Hal Finney)
Subject: Extortion Countermeasures in Crypto-Anarchy

I missed the earlier debate on this topic that Tim mentioned, so please
excuse me if I'm rehashing old arguments.

I don't think the strategy of ignoring extortion threats by pretending
that they were lost in the "noise" will work.  As Tim says, extorters
can make their threats very credible.  For example, before burning down
a factory, an extorter can publically post an encrypted, signed message
describing what will happen.  Afterwards, the key is posted, allowing
the message to be read.  Any future message signed by that key will be
known to be by someone who is capable of and willing to commit property
damage.

Even if there are many extortion threats, only a few will be signed with
keys which have such credentials.  It will be in everyone's best interest
not to ignore such threats.  Imagine that you are in an alley, and a
man comes up to you, puts a gun to your head, and demands your money.  Will
you walk on, ignoring him, pretending you had too much on your mind to
pay attention?  Most people won't.  Ignoring the few credible extortion
threats by pretending you didn't notice them among the non-credible ones
would be a comparable strategy.

Not only is threatening extortion made easier, carrying it out becomes
easier, too.  Thugs-for-hire can advertise openly, using similar tech-
niques to establish their credentials (posting about crimes ahead of time,
with decryption keys released afterwards).  I'd imagine that the general
approach of paying 50% in advance, 50% upon completion will allow relationships
to form; in time, reputations among this virtual underworld will develop
to make hiring criminals even easier.  But everyone would still be strictly
anonymous to everyone else.

It's true that today there are crimes which might be easy, but which most
people forego.  But the opportunities of the future will be safer, and
in many cases will involve only property damage, with no human lives at
stake.  How many environmentalists would refrain from committing property
damage against Exxon if they could do so with perfect safety?  I'll bet
there are a lot of hotheads who'd take action.  Why, right here on this
list I'll bet there are people who would take illegal action against the
State.  Others have equal hatreds against their personal demonized groups.

My worry is that "crypto-anarchy", rather than being the somewhat "tamed"
anarchy we usually discuss here in the context of private law and such,
may come to resemble more the popular conception of anarchy, a constant
tooth-and-nail fight of all against all.  Cryptography will provide a
nearly perfect shield behind which all but the lowest-rung criminals can
hide, and in such a system justice is impossible.

