Date: Tue, 5 Oct 93 17:00:35 EDT
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Extortion Countermeasures in Crypto-Anarchy

Hal Finney writes:
> My worry is that "crypto-anarchy", rather than being the somewhat "tamed"
> anarchy we usually discuss here in the context of private law and such,
> may come to resemble more the popular conception of anarchy, a constant
> tooth-and-nail fight of all against all.  Cryptography will provide a
> nearly perfect shield behind which all but the lowest-rung criminals can
> hide, and in such a system justice is impossible.

This is definitely a potent worry, but I don't see realiable crypto
giving criminals the perfect anonymity described. Doubtless, the
widespread use of crypto would render it impossible to know who sent a
particular message, but it does nothing to alter the chances that the
police (or their PPL substitutes) will apprehend the perpetrator the
old-fashioned way: by simply examining the physical evidence.

Case in point: a little more than a week ago in Boston, a cop was shot
in his car while on undercover duty. Based on a single eyewitness
report of a brown VW Rabbit, the police have apprehended a suspect,
barely nine days later.

Now, it is certainly the case that the police are more diligent in
their work when one of their own has been the victim, but the point
is clear anyway: this perpetrator sent no messages and had to arrange
no payment: his "crypto" anonymity could not have been better. Yet
the physical evidence and reports of witnesses did him in anyway. These
risks would in no way have been diminished if he had used crypto in
the crime.

I don't doubt that extortion will occur in a state of Crypto-Anarchy,
but I don't expect it to be a greater problem than it is today, simply
because although the risks of making a threat decrease, the risks of
carrying it out remain the same.

